WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.920
Prompt engineering is the process of designing and testing the instruction and context package given to a language model.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:16.340
That package may include the task, background information, constraints, examples, source material, tool descriptions, and output requirements.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.280
A prompt is not just a sentence at the end of a chat.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:28.620
In an application, it is often an interface contract between user intent, available context, model behavior, and the output that another person or system

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.880
will review.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:32.740
Language model output is probabilistic.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:40.720
The model predicts likely continuations based on training, current instructions, available context, settings, and application configuration.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:47.820
Output may vary across runs, models, sampling settings, system configurations, retrieved content, and tool results.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:54.520
A better prompt can improve consistency and usefulness, but it cannot guarantee truth, safety, or perfect formatting.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:01:00.660
That is why prompt engineering should be treated as disciplined design work, not a collection of magic phrases.

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.120
A polished answer may still be wrong.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.900
A confident answer may still lack evidence.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.440
A prompt that works for one model or product workflow may behave differently in another.

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:17.620
Good prompt engineering makes the intended behavior clearer, then tests whether the behavior actually appears across representative cases.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:26.000
In practice, a dependable AI workflow combines prompting with verification, application controls, and human review where consequences matter.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.020
Source support may be required for factual claims.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:36.920
Authorization, validation, logging, rate limiting, output filtering, and approval gates remain application responsibilities.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.220
Prompting helps specify behavior.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.040
It does not replace engineering controls or accountable decision-making.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.620
This framing also helps teams avoid anthropomorphism.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.880
The model is not deciding like a person with stable intent.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.340
It is generating likely text or tool requests within a configured system.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:01.520
A prompt can guide that generation by making the task, context, boundaries, and output expectations explicit.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:07.980
The more important the workflow, the more the prompt should be treated like a tested software artifact rather than an informal message.
